name: "TUI Benchmark"

# ============================================================================
# WITHDRAWN. THIS FILE IS NOT A WORKFLOW AND IS NOT RUN BY ANYTHING.
# ============================================================================
#
# It is parked here, under `ci/benchmarks/` and with a `.withdrawn` suffix, so
# that the reasoning below survives. Moving it to
# `.github/workflows/tui-benchmark.yml` is what activates it, and DOING THAT IS
# A PIPELINE DECISION THAT HAS NOT BEEN TAKEN — see "WHY IT WAS WITHDRAWN".
#
# The suffix is load-bearing in two ways, both mechanical:
#
#   * `.github/workflows/*.yml` is the ONLY root set `ci/test/shell-gate-coverage.sh`
#     seeds reachability from. A file here seeds nothing, so nothing this file
#     names is credited as CI-reachable — which is the correct answer, because
#     CI does not reach it.
#   * The name does not end in `.yml`, so no `*.yml` glob — a linter's, a
#     workflow's `paths:` filter, a future collector's — picks it up by accident.
#
# The split logic below is written INLINE in its `run:` block rather than
# extracted to `ci/benchmarks/split-artifact.sh`, and that is deliberate too:
# `shell-gate-coverage.sh` discovers every `*.sh`, `*.mjs` and `*.py` under
# `ci/` and `scripts/` as a gate, and a script that only a WITHDRAWN workflow
# calls is reachable from no lane at all. Extracting it would create a dark gate
# and redden the very guard this file was withdrawn to respect.
#
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# WHAT HAS AND HAS NOT BEEN VALIDATED, stated plainly because this file has
# never run
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
#
# VALIDATED, by execution, against the committed
# `bench-results/benchmark_results.json` on 2026-09-06:
#
#   * The `jq` split below. Run against the real artifact it divides 12 entries
#     into 11 + 1, with `tui/strip-cache-hit-rate` alone on the
#     bigger-is-better side. Both halves non-empty; the counts sum to 12.
#   * That the split key is UNAMBIGUOUS on that artifact: of the 12 entries,
#     exactly 11 carry a `target<=` token and exactly 1 carries `target>=`.
#     None carries both and none carries neither, which is the property the
#     sum-to-the-whole assertion exists to keep true as metrics are added.
#   * That parking the file here leaves `ci/test/shell-gate-coverage.sh` green.
#
# NOT VALIDATED, because it cannot be from here, and NOT claimed:
#
#   * That a runner matching `[self-hosted, benchmark]` exists and is idle.
#   * That `benchmark-action/github-action-benchmark@v1` behaves as documented
#     for two data directories on one `gh-pages` branch.
#   * That the `nix develop --command just …` steps succeed on a runner. They
#     are the recipes this repository uses locally; nothing has run them in CI.
#
# Anyone activating this file owns those three. The first `gh-pages` baseline
# run is where they get answered.
#
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# WHY IT WAS WITHDRAWN
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
#
# `just bench` depends on `just build-tui`, which depends on
# `scripts/build-tui-grammars.sh`, which declares `NOT-A-CI-GATE:` in its own
# header. `ci/test/shell-gate-coverage.sh` refuses that combination by name —
# "is reachable from CI AND declares 'NOT-A-CI-GATE:' — one of the two is
# wrong" — and it is right to. Putting the TUI benchmark in a pipeline puts the
# TUI BUILD in one, and that moves the `tui` and `tui-real-terminal` lanes from
# "declared, green, waiting on a pipeline" to "in CI". That is a decision that
# belongs with the change that publishes this campaign's branches, which is also
# what a `gh-pages` baseline needs before the comparison step means anything.
#
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# CTUI-14. The eight §8 performance targets of
# codetracer-specs/Front-Ends/CodeTracer-TUI.md, plus a time-to-debugger entry
# and a release-build figure for the fuzzy command palette.
#
# Follows metacraft-dev-guidelines/policies/continuous-benchmarking.md §4 and
# the reference workflow it names
# (codetracer-native-recorder/.github/workflows/benchmark.yml).
#
# ## THE MEASUREMENT JOB IS ON THE DEDICATED BENCHMARK RUNNER, and that is the
# ## whole reason this is a separate workflow
#
# The policy is explicit: "benchmark measurement jobs must use the self-hosted
# runner pool … never GitHub-hosted runners. Concurrent CI traffic on shared
# runners makes perf measurements unreliable." `gpu-server-001` is CPU-isolated
# and is a single instance, so this workflow's concurrency group is what keeps
# two runs off it at once — a queued run is correct here and a cancelled one is
# not, because a benchmark that raced another benchmark is a number nobody can
# use.
#
# This was measured rather than assumed while CTUI-14 was written: on a 24-core
# developer host that was also running a self-hosted runner, the identical
# release binary reported `tui/time-to-debugger` at 259.7 ms at load 38.6 and at
# 82.5 ms at load 17.0 within four minutes. Every entry the suite writes carries
# its own `load1`, so a figure taken on a busy machine can be recognised as one.
#
# ## gh-pages TRACKING IS NOT WIRED YET, deliberately
#
# `benchmark-action/github-action-benchmark` compares against a baseline stored
# on `gh-pages`. There is no baseline until this workflow has run on the default
# branch at least once, and the CodeTracer TUI campaign's work is unpushed
# across six repositories at the time this file was written. The PR leg below is
# therefore `save-data-file: false` / `auto-push: false` (a comment, no write)
# and the baseline leg is guarded on the default branch, which is exactly the
# shape the policy's reference workflow uses. Nothing here needs changing when
# the first baseline lands.

on:
  push:
    branches: ["main"]
    paths:
      - "src/frontend/tui/**"
      - "src/frontend/viewmodel/**"
      - "justfile"
      - ".github/workflows/tui-benchmark.yml"
  pull_request:
    paths:
      - "src/frontend/tui/**"
      - "src/frontend/viewmodel/**"
      - "justfile"
      - ".github/workflows/tui-benchmark.yml"
  workflow_dispatch:

concurrency:
  # ONE AT A TIME ON THE BENCHMARK RUNNER, and a queued run is not cancelled.
  # `cancel-in-progress: false` is the opposite of what most workflows want and
  # is right here: killing a half-finished measurement wastes the slot and
  # leaves no number, while queuing costs only time.
  group: tui-benchmark-${{ github.ref }}
  cancel-in-progress: false

jobs:
  benchmark:
    runs-on: [self-hosted, benchmark]
    timeout-minutes: 60
    steps:
      - uses: actions/checkout@v5
        with:
          submodules: recursive

      # `just bench` depends on `build-tui`, which depends on `tui-prereqs`
      # (submodules + the ten-grammar archive). Four of the metrics are
      # properties of the SHIPPED PROCESS and are measured by spawning it in a
      # real pty, so the binary is not optional.
      - name: Build the TUI and the benchmark binary
        run: nix develop --command just build-tui-benchmarks

      # The recorded fixtures the in-process half opens. A missing fixture
      # FAILS the run by name rather than skipping — the suite raises with the
      # recipe that produces one.
      - name: Warm the fixture corpus
        run: nix develop --command just test-tui

      - name: Run benchmarks
        run: nix develop --command just bench --quick

      - name: Upload report
        if: always()
        uses: actions/upload-artifact@v4
        with:
          name: tui-benchmark-report
          path: bench-results/

      # ══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════
      # THE ARTIFACT IS SPLIT BY COMPARISON DIRECTION BEFORE IT IS PUBLISHED
      # ══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════
      #
      # `benchmark-action/github-action-benchmark` takes ONE `tool:` PER OUTPUT
      # FILE, and that setting is what decides whether a RISE is a regression.
      # This suite mixes senses: eleven of its twelve entries are
      # smaller-is-better, and `tui/strip-cache-hit-rate` is a HIT RATE whose §8
      # target is `>= 92%`.
      #
      # An earlier draft of this file set `tool: customSmallerIsBetter` for the
      # whole artifact. Restored as written it would have alerted on the strip
      # cache GETTING BETTER, and reported a collapse from 98% to 5% as an
      # improvement — alerting with the sign inverted, on the one metric here
      # whose failure is silent in every other respect (the shipped driver's
      # rate is a property nothing else in the suite observes).
      #
      # THE SPLIT KEY IS A FACT THE ARTIFACT CARRIES, not a list of metric names
      # kept in step by hand. The suite already writes `target<=<n>` or
      # `target>=<n>` into every entry's `extra` — it is how the report renders
      # the comparison column — so the direction is read back out of the data.
      # A hand-maintained name list would go stale the first time a second
      # bigger-is-better metric is added, and would do so SILENTLY, which is the
      # same failure one level up.
      #
      # BOTH HALVES ARE ASSERTED NON-EMPTY AND ASSERTED TO SUM TO THE WHOLE.
      # A `jq` filter that matches nothing produces `[]`, which the action
      # ACCEPTS and reports as a run with no benchmarks — trap 4 of
      # `codetracer-specs/Testing/Verification-Harness-Traps.md`, arriving in a
      # workflow. The sum assertion catches the other direction: an entry whose
      # `extra` carries neither token, or both, would be silently dropped from
      # or double-counted across the two charts.
      - name: Split the artifact by comparison direction
        run: |
          set -euo pipefail

          artifact=bench-results/benchmark_results.json
          smaller_file=bench-results/benchmark_results.smaller-is-better.json
          bigger_file=bench-results/benchmark_results.bigger-is-better.json

          jq '[.[] | select(.extra | test("target<=[0-9]"))]' \
            "${artifact}" > "${smaller_file}"
          jq '[.[] | select(.extra | test("target>=[0-9]"))]' \
            "${artifact}" > "${bigger_file}"

          total=$(jq 'length' "${artifact}")
          smaller=$(jq 'length' "${smaller_file}")
          bigger=$(jq 'length' "${bigger_file}")

          echo "artifact carries ${total} entrie(s):"
          echo "  ${smaller} smaller-is-better -> perf/tui"
          jq -r '.[] | "    " + .name' "${smaller_file}"
          echo "  ${bigger} bigger-is-better -> perf/tui-bigger"
          jq -r '.[] | "    " + .name' "${bigger_file}"

          # An empty half is the trap: the action accepts `[]` and reports a
          # run with no benchmarks, so the chart quietly stops updating.
          if [ "${smaller}" -eq 0 ]; then
            echo "::error::the smaller-is-better half is EMPTY — the action would accept it as a run with no benchmarks" >&2
            exit 1
          fi
          if [ "${bigger}" -eq 0 ]; then
            echo "::error::the bigger-is-better half is EMPTY — tui/strip-cache-hit-rate should be here; has its target direction changed?" >&2
            exit 1
          fi

          # Nothing dropped and nothing counted twice.
          if [ "$((smaller + bigger))" -ne "${total}" ]; then
            echo "::error::${smaller} + ${bigger} != ${total} — an entry's extra carries neither 'target<=' nor 'target>=', or carries both" >&2
            exit 1
          fi

      # ── PULL REQUEST: comment, never write the baseline ──────────────────
      #
      # TWO INVOCATIONS PER LEG, one per direction, into TWO DATA DIRECTORIES.
      # `perf/tui-bigger` is its own directory rather than a second series in
      # `perf/tui` because two series with opposite senses in one chart is the
      # same defect one level up: the chart's own axis would mean "lower" for
      # eleven lines and "higher" for one.
      - name: Compare benchmarks — smaller is better (PR)
        if: github.event_name == 'pull_request'
        uses: benchmark-action/github-action-benchmark@v1
        with:
          tool: customSmallerIsBetter
          output-file-path: bench-results/benchmark_results.smaller-is-better.json
          gh-pages-branch: gh-pages
          benchmark-data-dir-path: perf/tui
          # The org default. The SUITE also writes a per-metric
          # `alert-threshold=` into each entry's `extra`, derived from that
          # metric's own headroom against its published target and clamped to
          # [105%, 120%] — a metric sitting at half its budget can absorb the
          # default and one sitting at 95% of it cannot. The action takes one
          # number for the whole file, so this is the ceiling and the per-metric
          # figures are the reviewer's context.
          alert-threshold: "120%"
          comment-always: true
          save-data-file: false
          auto-push: false

      - name: Compare benchmarks — bigger is better (PR)
        if: github.event_name == 'pull_request'
        uses: benchmark-action/github-action-benchmark@v1
        with:
          tool: customBiggerIsBetter
          output-file-path: bench-results/benchmark_results.bigger-is-better.json
          gh-pages-branch: gh-pages
          benchmark-data-dir-path: perf/tui-bigger
          alert-threshold: "120%"
          comment-always: true
          save-data-file: false
          auto-push: false

      # ── DEFAULT BRANCH: update the baseline ──────────────────────────────
      - name: Update baseline — smaller is better (main)
        if: github.ref == 'refs/heads/main'
        uses: benchmark-action/github-action-benchmark@v1
        with:
          tool: customSmallerIsBetter
          output-file-path: bench-results/benchmark_results.smaller-is-better.json
          gh-pages-branch: gh-pages
          benchmark-data-dir-path: perf/tui
          auto-push: true

      - name: Update baseline — bigger is better (main)
        if: github.ref == 'refs/heads/main'
        uses: benchmark-action/github-action-benchmark@v1
        with:
          tool: customBiggerIsBetter
          output-file-path: bench-results/benchmark_results.bigger-is-better.json
          gh-pages-branch: gh-pages
          benchmark-data-dir-path: perf/tui-bigger
          auto-push: true
